#!/bin/sh
# Dodona enforcement layer 2: git itself refuses a commit made from the SHARED CHECKOUT.
#
# This file is the tracked, reviewable SOURCE. tools/dev.ps1 copies it into the repository's
# common .git/hooks on every run, because .git/hooks is not versioned and an install step
# someone has to remember is not enforcement (CLAUDE.md 0.3 / D-6). Same move DeployGate makes
# with .git/info/exclude: deploy it, do not ask.
#
# `git config core.hooksPath .githooks` looks like the better answer and was implemented, then
# MEASURED FAILING: a commit from the shared checkout succeeded against it. The hooks directory
# is TRACKED, so it only exists on branches and commits that carry it -- every other branch, and
# every historical commit you check out while bisecting, silently has no hook at all. A lock
# that disappears when you change branches is worse than a copy in .git. The copy is
# branch-independent, which is the property that matters here.
#
# WHY THIS IS THE ONLY LOCK. There was a second: a PreToolUse hook refusing Edit/Write into
# the shared checkout, plus a sibling refusing `git add -A`. Both were removed, on measurement
# rather than argument. They cost 255 ms on EVERY edit and EVERY shell command (136 ms of that
# is merely starting PowerShell) -- a permanent tax on all work for rules that should fire
# approximately never -- and they could not see a heredoc, sed or a redirect anyway, so they
# were never a guarantee.
#
# What they guarded is now structural: once every session works in its OWN worktree, a broad
# `git add` can only stage that session's own files. The mechanism behind f9aaf25 -- two
# sessions sharing one working tree, `git add` unable to tell whose edit was whose -- is
# prevented by the separation itself.
#
# So: CLAUDE.md 0.0 says where to work, and THIS refuses the one act that makes a mistake
# permanent. Git runs it with no agent cooperation, no choice of tool evades it, and it costs
# about 40 ms once per commit.
#
# THE TEST, same stateless one as layer 1: git leaves `.git` as a FILE in a worktree and a
# DIRECTORY in the main checkout. No registry, no lock, nothing that can go stale or jam.
#
# SCOPED TO THIS REPOSITORY ON PURPOSE. Never `git config --global core.hooksPath`: all 11
# suites build temp repos and run `git commit -m init` in them, every one of those a main
# checkout, and a global install would abort all of them. Checked before installing.

# The one deliberate override (D-7 item 4), same variable as layer 1: sometimes the shared
# checkout is genuinely right -- a release commit, or installing these hooks. A choice you
# make, never a wall you hit (CLAUDE.md 0.1).
if [ "$DODONA_ALLOW_MAIN_TREE" = "1" ]; then
    exit 0
fi

top=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null) || exit 0
[ -n "$top" ] || exit 0

# A FILE means worktree, which is where work belongs. Only a DIRECTORY is the shared checkout.
if [ ! -d "$top/.git" ]; then
    exit 0
fi

name="w$(date +%m%d-%H%M)"
cat >&2 <<MSG

COMMIT REFUSED -- this is the shared checkout ($top), not a worktree.

Two sessions in one checkout cannot tell whose edit is whose, and this is the boundary
where that becomes permanent: f9aaf25 committed another lane's lanes.cwd migration and
Ver.cs changes along with its own routing fix, because both sat in the same working tree.

Move the work to a tree of its own:
    powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File tools/dev.ps1 worktree $name
    git -C .claude/worktrees/$name ...      # commit from there

If the shared checkout is genuinely the right place (a release commit, installing hooks):
    DODONA_ALLOW_MAIN_TREE=1 git commit ...

This is Dodona enforcement layer 2 (RECOVERY-PHASES D-7). Layer 1 is the PreToolUse hook
in .claude/settings.json; this one exists because layer 1 cannot see a shell redirect.
MSG
exit 1
